<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferdinand III, Holy Roman Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1608">1608</a>–<a href="/years/1657">1657</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferdinand III, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg dynasty</a>, reigned from 1637 to 1657. He commanded the imperial army at Nördlingen in 1634 and, as emperor, sought to end the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, accepting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648. Like other emperors he raised loyal nobles in rank, creating new imperial duchies and counties.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Ferdinand III, Holy Roman Emperor" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan van den Hoecke · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jan_van_den_Hoecke_-_Portrait_of_Emperor_Ferdinand_III.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ferdinand was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a> on 13 July 1608, the third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Ferdinand II, Holy Roman Emperor</a>, and Maria Anna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and was baptised Ferdinand Ernst. He grew up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> and received religious and scholarly training from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>. After the deaths of his brothers Karl in 1603 and Johann Karl in 1619, he was designated his father's successor. He became Archduke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1621, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> on 8 December 1625 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-bohemia">King of Bohemia</a> on 27 November 1627. In 1631 he married his cousin Maria Anna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-wallenstein">Albrecht von Wallenstein</a>'s assassination, Ferdinand took command of the imperial army on 2 May 1634. He regained Regensburg in July and Donauwörth in August, and in September won the decisive Battle of Nördlingen with Spanish forces under Cardinal Infante Ferdinand, although his personal part in it was limited. He was elected King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> on 22 December 1636 and became emperor on his father's death on 15 February 1637, when much of the empire had been devastated by two decades of war. By 1640 he had permanently lost control of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p>Peace negotiations began in Münster and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a> in 1644 and lasted until 1648 while fighting went on. He suffered defeat at Jankau on 6 March 1645, after which a Swedish army advanced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. In the Peace of Linz of 1645 he guaranteed the Hungarian estates freedom of religion for Protestants. Spanish support ceased in 1645, and his instructions to his chief negotiator, Maximilian von Trauttmansdorff, show that he was ready to surrender more than was in the end necessary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> cost the empire territory: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-bishoprics">Three Bishoprics</a> went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> received Rügen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Western Pomerania</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> gained full independence. The imperial estates won the right to form alliances with foreign powers, and Ferdinand's attempt at absolutist rule of the empire failed. In his own lands his power was greater than his predecessors' before 1618. He restructured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulic-council">Aulic Council</a> and summoned the last traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> in 1652.</p><p>Ferdinand died on 2 April 1657 and rests in the Capuchin Crypt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. His son Leopold I succeeded him after the death of his elder son Ferdinand IV in 1654. He was a patron of music and the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarch recognised as a composer; some of his masses, motets and hymns survive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ferdinand III, Holy Roman Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Thirty Years' War, imperial and Spanish forces under the future Emperor <b>Ferdinand III</b> and the Cardinal-Infante Ferdinand crushed the Swedes and their German allies under Gustav Horn and Bernhard of Saxe-Weimar.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-6">6 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1650 <b>Emperor Ferdinand III</b> raised him to imperial count and made Barmstedt an immediate imperial county, outside the duchy of Holstein.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-rantzau">County of Rantzau</a></li><li><blockquote>It became a princely county in 1576, and in 1644 <b>Emperor Ferdinand III</b> raised it to a duchy under Philippe-Charles d'Arenberg.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-arenberg">Duchy of Arenberg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.07,15.44&amp;year=1639" aria-label="Graz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4912 511 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ferdinand III, Holy Roman Emperor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5362 781h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5362 781h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.07,15.44&amp;year=1639">What was here in 1639? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
